N,R,S="N","R","S"
SCENES={
"title":[(N,"Operation Gate. The Thinking Machine. Artificial intelligence for GATE Data Science and A I, twenty twenty seven."),
(N,"Search, logic and uncertainty. Every idea is run in plain Python with comments, so you can see the logic and change it.")],
"unin":[(N,"Room one. Uninformed search."),
(R,"How does an agent find a path when it knows nothing about where the goal is?"),
(S,"It explores systematically. Breadth first search expands level by level using a queue. Depth first search dives using a stack. Uniform cost search always expands the cheapest path so far, using a priority queue."),
(S,"The code has a start S and a goal G. Both paths have two edges. The path through A costs seven. The path through B costs five."),
(S,"Breadth first search returns S, A, G with cost seven, because it counts edges and ignores cost. Uniform cost returns S, B, G with cost five. Only uniform cost is optimal when steps have different costs."),
(N,"Twist one. With branching factor b and depth d, the tree has about b to the d nodes. Ten branches, depth five, is a hundred thousand nodes.")],
"inf":[(N,"Room two. Informed search."),
(R,"Can the agent use a hint about the goal?"),
(S,"Yes, a heuristic h of n, an estimate of the remaining cost. A star expands the node with the smallest f, which is cost so far g, plus h. Greedy search uses h alone and can be fooled."),
(S,"The code finds a path through a small maze. Uniform cost expands twenty three nodes. A star with the Manhattan distance expands only nineteen, and both find a path of length ten."),
(S,"The guarantee needs an admissible heuristic, one that never overestimates the true cost. In the second graph, the honest heuristic returns four. If we claim that B is expensive, ten instead of two, the search wrongly returns six."),
(N,"Twist two. An overestimating heuristic hides the best path. Admissible gives optimal tree search, and consistent makes graph search optimal too.")],
"adv":[(N,"Room three. Adversarial search."),
(R,"What if an opponent is trying to beat me?"),
(S,"Use minimax. I pick the move that maximises my score, assuming the opponent picks the reply that minimises it. Levels alternate between max and min."),
(S,"The code builds a tree with eight leaves. Plain minimax looks at all eight leaves and returns five. Alpha beta returns the same five but looks at only five leaves."),
(S,"Alpha is the best value I can already guarantee. Beta is the best the opponent can guarantee. When alpha reaches beta, the rest of that branch cannot matter, so we prune it."),
(N,"Twist three. Alpha beta never changes the answer, only the work. With perfect move ordering it needs b to the d over two nodes, doubling the depth you can search.")],
"prop":[(N,"Room four. Propositional logic."),
(R,"How does a machine know something follows from what it knows?"),
(S,"A knowledge base entails a sentence if the sentence is true in every model where the knowledge base is true. Model checking tries every assignment of true and false."),
(S,"The code defines P implies Q, and P. It checks all four models. In every one where both hold, Q is true, so it prints true. That is modus ponens."),
(S,"Remove the fact P, and the answer becomes false, because P false makes the implication true even when Q is false. A false premise implies anything."),
(N,"Twist four. Model checking costs two to the n for n symbols. Resolution on conjunctive normal form is the practical proof method, and it is sound and complete.")],
"pred":[(N,"Room five. Predicate logic."),
(R,"Propositions are too small. How do we talk about everyone and someone?"),
(S,"Predicate logic adds objects, relations and quantifiers. For all x, and there exists y. Ann loves Bob, Bob loves Cy, Cy loves Ann is three facts about a relation."),
(S,"The code checks two sentences on that world. Everyone loves someone is true. There is someone loved by everyone is false. Same words, swapped quantifiers, different meaning."),
(S,"Unification makes two sentences match by binding variables. A variable x unifies with Ann by x equals Ann. Two different constants, Ann and Bob, cannot unify, so it returns none."),
(N,"Twist five. Order of quantifiers matters. For all, then exists, is not the same as exists, then for all. First order logic inference is only semi decidable.")],
"unc":[(N,"Room six. Reasoning under uncertainty."),
(R,"The world is noisy. Alarms ring without burglars. What then?"),
(S,"A Bayesian network gives each variable a table conditioned on its parents. Burglary is rare, point one percent. Earthquake is point two percent. The alarm depends on both. John and Mary each call depending on the alarm."),
(S,"The question is: given that both John and Mary called, what is the chance of a burglary? The joint probability is a product of the table entries along the network."),
(S,"Variable elimination sums out the hidden variables, earthquake and alarm, one at a time, instead of listing every combination. The code does the same sums and prints point two eight four."),
(S,"Sampling approximates it. Likelihood weighting fixes the evidence, samples the rest, and weights each sample by how well it fits the evidence. A hundred thousand samples give about point two eight."),
(N,"Twist six. Even with both calls, a burglary is only twenty eight percent likely, because burglaries are so rare. Sampling estimates also wobble with the random seed when the event is rare.")],
"wk":[(N,"Walkthrough. Watch alpha beta prune a branch, line by line."),
(S,"The leaves are three, five, two, nine. The root is a max node, with two min nodes below it."),
(S,"The first min node takes the smaller of three and five, which is three. So alpha becomes three: I can already guarantee three."),
(S,"The second min node looks at its first leaf, two. Two is already below three, so this node can only be two or less. The root will never pick it. The leaf nine is never looked at."),
(N,"Main logic. Keep the best guaranteed value, and stop exploring any branch that cannot beat it.")],
"end":[(N,"The Thinking Machine is awake. You can search with and without hints, play against an opponent, prove with logic, and reason with probabilities."),
(N,"Seven subjects, seven films. Good luck with GATE twenty twenty seven."),
(N,"Operation Gate complete.")],
}
